        "section": "pathology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Inflammation and Tissue Repair",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Three facts from this diagram organize the guide. First, **acute and chronic inflammation are\ndifferent programs**, not merely early and late versions of one process — different dominant\ncells, different mediators, different tissue outcomes. Second, **the outcome is a fork**: the\nsame acute response can resolve cleanly, wall off into an abscess, smolder into chronic\ninflammation, or heal by scar, depending on the agent, the tissue, and whether the stimulus\npersists. Third, **repair is either regeneration or scar**, and which one a tissue gets is\nlargely decided by two things — whether its cells can divide, and whether the supporting\nscaffold survived."
    }
